{"course_metadata":{"course_name":"Linear Algebra","course_code":"MATH2059","course_level":"UG","credit_hours":3,"domain":"Mathematics","institution":"","semester":"","prerequisites":["Discrete Mathematics","Advanced Engineering Mathematics 1","Advanced Engineering Mathematics 2"]},"topics":[{"topic_name":"Introduction to Linear Algebra","unit_number":1,"learning_intent":"Students will understand the fundamental distinction between linear and non-linear systems, recognize the scope of linear algebra as a discipline, and identify real-world applications where linear algebraic methods are essential tools.","total_hours":1.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Linearity versus Non-linearity","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.4,"description":"Definition of linearity through the superposition principle (additivity and homogeneity). Examination of common non-linear phenomena in nature and engineering that linear algebra approximates. Introduction to the concept of linear systems as idealized models with tractable solutions.","prerequisites_within_course":[]},{"subtopic_name":"Real-life Examples and Applications Overview","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.3,"description":"Survey of application domains including Google PageRank algorithm, Google Maps navigation, computer graphics, machine learning, and data science. Students will see how linear algebra provides the mathematical backbone for these technologies.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linearity versus Non-linearity"]},{"subtopic_name":"Scope and Organization of the Course","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.3,"description":"Roadmap of the syllabus covering systems of equations, vector spaces, eigenvalues, singular value decomposition, and applications. Discussion of how these topics interconnect and build upon each other throughout the semester.","prerequisites_within_course":[]}]},{"topic_name":"System of Linear Equations","unit_number":2,"learning_intent":"Students will develop proficiency in representing and solving systems of linear equations using vector and matrix notation, understand the concepts of linear combinations and matrix rank, and apply these techniques to practical problems in machine learning, cryptography, and digital imaging.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Vectors and Linear Combinations","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Introduction to vectors in R^n including component representation, vector addition, and scalar multiplication. Definition of linear combinations as sums of scaled vectors. Geometric interpretation of vectors in two and three dimensions as arrows and points. Applications to mixing problems and resource allocation.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linearity versus Non-linearity"]},{"subtopic_name":"Geometric Visualization of Linear Systems","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Graphical representation of two and three variable linear equations as lines and planes. Interpretation of solution sets as intersections. Classification of systems as having unique solutions, infinitely many solutions, or no solution based on geometric configuration.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and Linear Combinations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Gaussian Elimination and Row Reduction","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Systematic procedure for solving linear systems using elementary row operations. Introduction to augmented matrices and row echelon form. Back-substitution method for obtaining solutions. Treatment of special cases including inconsistent systems and free variables.","prerequisites_within_course":["Geometric Visualization of Linear Systems"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix Rank and Echelon Forms","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Definition of matrix rank as the number of non-zero rows in row echelon form. Relationship between rank and number of pivots. Row reduced echelon form (RREF) and its uniqueness property. Rank-nullity relationship for homogeneous systems.","prerequisites_within_course":["Gaussian Elimination and Row Reduction"]},{"subtopic_name":"Homogeneous and Non-homogeneous Systems","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Complete characterization of solution sets for homogeneous systems including the trivial solution and parametric representation of infinite solutions. Non-homogeneous systems using particular solutions and homogeneous solutions. Connection to vector spaces and null spaces.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix Rank and Echelon Forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications to Machine Learning, Cryptography, and Color Models","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.5,"description":"Practical applications including linear regression formulation as a linear system, Hill cipher encryption using matrix operations, and RGB color model representation as vectors. Worked examples demonstrating real-world relevance of solution techniques.","prerequisites_within_course":["Homogeneous and Non-homogeneous Systems"]}]},{"topic_name":"Vector Spaces and Linear Transformations","unit_number":3,"learning_intent":"Students will master the abstract framework of vector spaces, understand linear transformations as structure-preserving mappings, apply the rank-nullity theorem, perform change of basis computations, and utilize inner product spaces with orthogonal projection techniques.","total_hours":12.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Vector Spaces and Subspaces","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Axiomatic definition of vector spaces over real and complex fields. Examples including R^n, polynomial spaces, and function spaces. Definition and characterization of subspaces as subsets closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Testing for subspace properties.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and Linear Combinations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear Span and Independence","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Definition of the span of a set of vectors as all linear combinations. Spanning sets and their geometric interpretation. Linear independence as minimal generating sets. Theorems relating independence, spanning, and basis. Wronskian determinant for function independence.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vector Spaces and Subspaces"]},{"subtopic_name":"Basis and Dimension","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Definition of basis as linearly independent spanning sets. Dimension theorem for finite-dimensional spaces. Standard bases for R^n and polynomial spaces. Coordinates of vectors relative to a basis. Extension of independent sets to bases.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear Span and Independence"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear Mappings, Kernel, and Image","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Definition of linear transformations between vector spaces. Kernel (null space) as the set of vectors mapping to zero. Image (range) as the set of output values. Properties including injectivity, surjectivity, and the relationship to matrix representation.","prerequisites_within_course":["Basis and Dimension"]},{"subtopic_name":"Rank-Nullity Theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Fundamental theorem relating dimensions of kernel and image for linear transformations. Proof using basis extension arguments. Applications to solving linear systems and understanding matrix properties. Connection to matrix rank.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear Mappings, Kernel, and Image"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix Representation, Similarity, and Change of Basis","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Matrix representation of linear transformations relative to chosen bases. Change of basis formula for matrices. Similar matrices representing the same transformation in different bases. Diagonalization concept preview.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank-Nullity Theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Inner Product Spaces, Norms, and Orthogonality","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Definition of inner product spaces extending Euclidean geometry to abstract spaces. Norm induced by inner product. Orthogonality and perpendicularity in general inner product spaces. Cauchy-Schwarz and triangle inequalities.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix Representation, Similarity, and Change of Basis"]},{"topic_name":"Gram-Schmidt Process and Applications","unit_number":3,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Systematic procedure for constructing orthogonal bases from arbitrary bases. Modified Gram-Schmidt for numerical stability. QR decomposition as a matrix formulation. Applications to least squares problems.","prerequisites_within_course":["Inner Product Spaces, Norms, and Orthogonality"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications to Data Smoothing and Image Scaling","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.5,"description":"Practical applications including polynomial fitting for data smoothing using orthogonal projections and bilinear interpolation in image processing using linear transformations. Worked examples connecting theory to implementation.","prerequisites_within_course":["Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization"]}],"learning_intent":"Students will master the abstract framework of vector spaces, understand linear transformations as structure-preserving mappings, apply the rank-nullity theorem, perform change of basis computations, and utilize inner product spaces with orthogonal projection techniques.","total_hours":12.0},{"topic_name":"Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","unit_number":4,"learning_intent":"Students will compute and interpret eigenvalues and eigenvectors for various matrix types, apply the Cayley-Hamilton theorem and diagonalization procedures, analyze symmetric matrices and quadratic forms, and utilize these concepts in least squares approximation and applications like facial recognition and search engines.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Characteristic Polynomial and Cayley-Hamilton Theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Definition of characteristic polynomial as det(A - λI). Computation of eigenvalues as roots of the characteristic polynomial. Cayley-Hamilton theorem stating every matrix satisfies its characteristic equation. Applications to computing matrix polynomials.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix Rank and Echelon Forms","Linear Mappings, Kernel, and Image"]},{"subtopic_name":"Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors: Computation","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Procedures for finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors for small matrices. Eigenspace definition as the nullspace of (A - λI). Geometric multiplicity versus algebraic multiplicity. Worked examples with 2x2 and 3x3 matrices.","prerequisites_within_course":["Characteristic Polynomial and Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Geometric Interpretation","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Visual interpretation of eigenvectors as directions unchanged by linear transformations. Eigenvalue sign indicating direction preservation or reversal. Scaling factors as eigenvalues. Applications to Markov chains and population modeling.","prerequisites_within_course":["Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors: Computation"]},{"subtopic_name":"Diagonalization and Matrix Functions","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Conditions for diagonalizability (complete sets of eigenvectors). Procedure for diagonalization: A = PDP^(-1). Computing powers of matrices using diagonalization. Functions of matrices including exponentials for differential equations.","prerequisites_within_course":["Geometric Interpretation"]},{"subtopic_name":"Symmetric Matrices and Quadratic Forms","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Special properties of symmetric matrices: real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors. Spectral theorem statement. Quadratic forms as x^T A x. Classification as positive definite, negative definite, or indefinite. Optimization connections.","prerequisites_within_course":["Diagonalization and Matrix Functions"]},{"subtopic_name":"Least Squares Method","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Normal equations for best approximation in overdetermined systems. Geometric interpretation using orthogonal projection. Solution using pseudoinverse and SVD connection. Applications to data fitting.","prerequisites_within_course":["Symmetric Matrices and Quadratic Forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications: Facial Recognition, Feature Extraction, and Search Engines","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.5,"description":"Practical applications including eigenfaces for facial recognition using PCA, feature extraction in pattern recognition, and PageRank using eigenvector centrality. Connection between theoretical concepts and implementation.","prerequisites_within_course":["Least Squares Method"]}]},{"topic_name":"Singular Value Decomposition","unit_number":5,"learning_intent":"Students will understand SVD as a generalization of eigenvalue decomposition for rectangular matrices, perform rank-k approximations, apply the power method for dominant singular vectors, and utilize SVD in principal component analysis, document ranking, and clustering algorithms.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Spectral Decomposition Review","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Review of eigendecomposition for symmetric matrices. Spectral theorem for symmetric matrices with orthogonal diagonalization. Limitations when matrices are non-square or non-symmetric. Motivation for SVD generalization.","prerequisites_within_course":["Symmetric Matrices and Quadratic Forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Singular Value Decomposition: Theory","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Definition of singular values and singular vectors. SVD decomposition A = UΣV^T. Relationship between singular vectors and eigenvectors of A^T A and AA^T. Geometric interpretation as sequential orthogonal transformations and scaling.","prerequisites_within_course":["Spectral Decomposition Review"]},{"subtopic_name":"Rank-k Approximation and the Power Method","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Low-rank approximation theorem: optimal k-rank approximation minimizes Frobenius norm. Eckart-Young theorem. Power method for computing dominant singular vectors. Iterative refinement and convergence analysis.","prerequisites_within_course":["Singular Value Decomposition: Theory"]},{"subtopic_name":"Principal Component Analysis","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"PCA as an application of SVD to centered data matrices. Variance maximization interpretation. Dimensionality reduction procedure. Selection of principal components using explained variance. Applications to data compression and visualization.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank-k Approximation and the Power Method"]},{"subtopic_name":"Document Ranking and Clustering with SVD","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Latent Semantic Analysis for document retrieval using term-document matrices. SVD-based clustering through k-means on principal components. Applications in search engines, recommendation systems, and text mining.","prerequisites_within_course":["Principal Component Analysis"]},{"subtopic_name":"SVD in Data Centering and Preprocessing","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Role of SVD in data preprocessing pipelines. Mean centering using matrix operations. Whitening transformations. Connections to covariance matrices and correlation structures in multivariate statistics.","prerequisites_within_course":["Document Ranking and Clustering with SVD"]}]},{"topic_name":"Applications of Linear Algebra","unit_number":6,"learning_intent":"Students will solve applied problems across diverse domains including traffic flow analysis, electrical circuits, chemical balancing, data compression, information retrieval, game theory, and genetics modeling, demonstrating the breadth of linear algebra as an essential tool in science and engineering.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Traffic Flow and Circuit Analysis","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Modeling traffic networks as linear systems using flow conservation equations. Application to street networks and roundabout optimization. Circuit analysis using Kirchhoff's laws leading to linear systems. Solving for currents and voltages in resistor networks.","prerequisites_within_course":["System of Linear Equations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Chemical Equation Balancing and Data Compression","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Balancing chemical equations as solving underdetermined linear systems. Mass conservation constraints. SVD-based image compression including quality versus compression ratio tradeoffs. Implementation considerations.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank-k Approximation and the Power Method"]},{"subtopic_name":"Markov Matrices and PageRank","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Introduction to stochastic matrices and Markov chains. Stationary distributions and eigenvalue properties. Google PageRank as eigenvector computation. Teleportation and damping factors. Convergence analysis.","prerequisites_within_course":["Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors: Computation","Spectral Decomposition Review"]},{"subtopic_name":"Game Strategies and Information Retrieval","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Matrix games and payoff matrices in game theory. Solution concepts using linear programming duality. Term-document matrices in information retrieval. TF-IDF weighting and cosine similarity for document ranking.","prerequisites_within_course":["Singular Value Decomposition: Theory"]},{"subtopic_name":"Word Embeddings, Bias in Data, and Chessboard Patterns","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Word embeddings using matrix factorization techniques including SVD-based methods. Detection and measurement of bias in data representations. King's moves on chessboards as linear transformations. Special matrix patterns in combinatorics.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear Mappings, Kernel, and Image"]},{"subtopic_name":"Genetics Modeling and Course Integration","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Population genetics using Leslie matrices for age-structured populations. Eigenvalue analysis for population growth rates. Integration of course concepts demonstrating interconnectedness of linear algebra topics. Review of key theoretical foundations and their applications.","prerequisites_within_course":["Markov Matrices and PageRank"]}]}],"total_hours":45.0,"coverage_summary":"This undergraduate Linear Algebra course provides a comprehensive introduction to the fundamental concepts and techniques of linear algebra with emphasis on computational proficiency and practical applications. Spanning 45 contact hours across six units, the syllabus progresses from foundational concepts of linearity and systems of linear equations through the abstract framework of vector spaces and linear transformations, culminating in advanced topics of eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and singular value decomposition. The curriculum balances theoretical understanding with extensive worked examples and applications drawn from machine learning, computer graphics, data science, search engine technology, and engineering domains. Students develop mathematical modeling skills using vectors, matrices, and linear systems, while gaining hands-on experience with computational techniques including Gaussian elimination, diagonalization, Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization, and SVD-based dimensionality reduction. The course prerequisites in discrete mathematics and advanced engineering mathematics ensure students possess the necessary mathematical maturity for rigorous reasoning while maintaining accessibility for undergraduates seeking to apply linear algebraic methods in computer and allied sciences."}
