ours":1.5,"description":"Introduction to n-dimensional vectors, vector addition, scalar multiplication, and linear combination of vectors. Geometric interpretation in 2D and 3D. Writing a system of equations as a vector equation and as a matrix-vector product.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linearity versus non-linearity"]},{"subtopic_name":"Visualization of linear systems","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Graphical representation of linear equations in two and three variables. Intersecting lines, planes, and the geometric meaning of unique, infinite, and no solutions. Row picture versus column picture of a system.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and linear combinations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix elimination and rank","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Gaussian elimination and Gauss-Jordan elimination as systematic solving procedures. Row operations, leading variables, free variables, and identification of the rank of a matrix. Connection between rank and the number of solutions.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and linear combinations","Visualization of linear systems"]},{"subtopic_name":"Echelon and normal forms","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Row echelon form and reduced row echelon form (RREF). Pivots, back-substitution, and converting any matrix to RREF. Elementary matrices and their role in representing row operations.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix elimination and rank"]},{"subtopic_name":"Homogeneous and non-homogeneous systems","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Distinction between homogeneous (Ax = 0) and non-homogeneous (Ax = b) systems. Null space (kernel) of a matrix. Solution sets: trivial, infinite, and empty. Conditions for consistency in non-homogeneous systems.","prerequisites_within_course":["Echelon and normal forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications of systems of linear equations","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":0.5,"description":"Applications in machine learning (linear regression setup), cryptography (encoding/decoding messages), and color models in digital imaging (RGB and CMY representations). Worked examples for each domain.","prerequisites_within_course":["Homogeneous and non-homogeneous systems"]}]},{"topic_name":"Vector Spaces and Linear Transformations","unit_number":3,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to define and work with vector spaces, identify subspaces, determine linear independence and basis, and apply linear transformations to map between vector spaces. They will understand the rank-nullity theorem and be able to compute orthonormal bases using the Gram-Schmidt process for applications in data smoothing and image scaling.","total_hours":12.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Vector spaces and subspaces","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Axiomatic definition of a vector space over R (and extension to C). Subspaces: definition, tests for subspace, examples (null space, column space, span of a set). Practical examples from R^n and function spaces.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vectors and linear combinations","Echelon and normal forms"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear span, independence, and basis","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":3.0,"description":"Linear combination and linear span of a set of vectors. Linear independence and dependence: definition, geometric interpretation, and connection to homogeneous systems. Spanning sets and minimal spanning sets. Basis of a vector space, coordinates of a vector relative to a basis, and the standard basis.","prerequisites_within_course":["Vector spaces and subspaces"]},{"subtopic_name":"Dimension and rank-nullity theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Dimension as the cardinality of any basis. Dimension theorem: dimension of a vector space equals the number of vectors in any basis. Rank-nullity theorem for linear transformations: dim(domain) = dim(kernel) + dim(image). Applications to solving linear systems.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear span, independence, and basis"]},{"subtopic_name":"Linear transformations, kernels, and images","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Definition of linear transformation between vector spaces. Kernel (null space) and image (range) of a linear transformation. One-to-one and onto transformations. Classification of linear transformations as singular or non-singular (invertible).","prerequisites_within_course":["Dimension and rank-nullity theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Matrix representations, similarity, and change of basis","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Matrix representation of a linear transformation given a basis. Similar matrices represent the same linear transformation in different bases. Change of basis matrix. Diagonal representation of a linear operator when possible. Introduction to matrix similarity.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear transformations, kernels, and images"]},{"subtopic_name":"Inner product spaces, orthogonality, and Gram-Schmidt","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Inner product on R^n and general inner product spaces. Norm induced by an inner product. Orthogonality and orthogonal complements. Orthogonal sets and orthonormal bases. Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process with worked numerical examples.","prerequisites_within_course":["Matrix representations, similarity, and change of basis"]}]},{"topic_name":"Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors","unit_number":4,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to compute eigenvalues and eigenvectors, diagonalize matrices, and apply these concepts to analyze symmetric matrices, classify quadratic forms, and solve least squares problems for applications in facial recognition, feature extraction, and search engines.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Characteristic polynomial and Cayley-Hamilton theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Characteristic equation det(A - λI) = 0. Trace and determinant in terms of eigenvalues. Multiplicity of eigenvalues (algebraic and geometric). Cayley-Hamilton theorem: every matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation, enabling evaluation of matrix polynomials.","prerequisites_within_course":["Linear transformations, kernels, and images"]},{"subtopic_name":"Eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and geometric interpretation","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Definition of eigenvector and eigenvalue. Geometric interpretation: eigenvectors as directions that remain unchanged (scaled) by the transformation. Visualization of stretching and rotation in R^2. Eigenspaces.","prerequisites_within_course":["Characteristic polynomial and Cayley-Hamilton theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Diagonalization and matrix powers","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Diagonalizable matrices: definition and sufficient condition (n linearly independent eigenvectors). Procedure for diagonalization A = PDP^{-1}. Computing A^k for large k using diagonalization. Functions of matrices such as e^{At} using diagonalization.","prerequisites_within_course":["Eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and geometric interpretation"]},{"subtopic_name":"Symmetric matrices and spectral theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Properties of symmetric matrices: real eigenvalues, orthogonal eigenvectors. Real spectral theorem for symmetric matrices: A = QDQ^T where Q is orthogonal. Connection between symmetry and diagonalization with orthogonal matrices.","prerequisites_within_course":["Diagonalization and matrix powers"]},{"subtopic_name":"Quadratic forms and least squares method","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.5,"description":"Quadratic forms x^T Ax. Change of variables to eliminate cross terms (principal axis theorem). Classification: positive definite, negative definite, indefinite. Least squares approximation: normal equations, projection onto subspace, and geometric interpretation. Applications in curve fitting and data fitting.","prerequisites_within_course":["Symmetric matrices and spectral theorem"]}]},{"topic_name":"Singular Value Decomposition","unit_number":5,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to perform and interpret singular value decomposition, use rank-k approximations to compress data, apply the power method for approximating dominant singular values, and employ PCA and related techniques for dimensionality reduction, document ranking, and clustering in data science contexts.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Spectral decomposition review","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Review of eigendecomposition for symmetric matrices. Limitations of eigenvalue decomposition for non-square and non-symmetric matrices. Need for a general matrix factorization. Connection between eigenvalues and singular values.","prerequisites_within_course":["Quadratic forms and least squares method"]},{"subtopic_name":"Singular value decomposition theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"SVD theorem: A = UΣV^T where U and V are orthogonal matrices and Σ is a diagonal matrix of singular values. Relationship between singular values and eigenvalues of A^TA. Construction of U and V from left and right singular vectors. Full and reduced SVD forms.","prerequisites_within_course":["Spectral decomposition review"]},{"subtopic_name":"Rank-k approximation and Eckart-Young theorem","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Low-rank approximation using truncated SVD. Eckart-Young theorem: best rank-k approximation in Frobenius norm is given by truncating SVD. Interpretation of singular values as energy in data. Application to image compression.","prerequisites_within_course":["Singular value decomposition theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Power method for singular values","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Power iteration for computing the dominant eigenvalue and eigenvector. Extension to singular values via A^TA. Convergence behavior, dominant singular value separation, and practical implementation. Limitations for ill-conditioned matrices.","prerequisites_within_course":["Rank-k approximation and Eckart-Young theorem"]},{"subtopic_name":"Applications: PCA, document ranking, and clustering","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Principal component analysis as an application of SVD: centering data, computing covariance matrix, extracting principal components. Data centering and its role in PCA. Document-term matrices and Latent Semantic Analysis for document ranking. Connection between SVD and k-means clustering.","prerequisites_within_course":["Power method for singular values"]}]},{"topic_name":"Applications of Linear Algebra","unit_number":6,"learning_intent":"Students will be able to apply linear algebra techniques across a range of engineering and data science domains, including traffic flow modeling, circuit analysis, chemical balancing, data compression, word embeddings, information retrieval, and genetic modeling. They will understand the role of Markov matrices in PageRank and can analyze game strategies using payoff matrices.","total_hours":8.0,"subtopics":[{"subtopic_name":"Engineering applications: traffic and circuits","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Formulation and solving of traffic flow problems using systems of linear equations (conservation of vehicles at junctions). Kirchhoff's laws and circuit analysis using linear systems. Worked examples for both applications.","prerequisites_within_course":["System of Linear Equations"]},{"subtopic_name":"Chemical equation balancing and data compression","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":1.0,"description":"Balancing chemical equations as a linear algebra problem (setting up and solving systems over integers). Data compression using SVD-based rank-k approximation on image and signal data, with comparison of compression ratio and reconstruction error.","prerequisites_within_course":["Chemical equation balancing and data compression"]},{"subtopic_name":"Word embeddings, bias in data, and Markov matrices","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Representation of words as vectors using embedding techniques and their properties (similarity, analogy). Detection and impact of bias in high-dimensional data through linear algebra. Markov matrices: transition matrices, steady-state vectors, and application to PageRank algorithm.","prerequisites_within_course":["Word embeddings, bias in data, and Markov matrices"]},{"subtopic_name":"Game strategies and information retrieval","depth_level":"intermediate","estimated_hours":1.5,"description":"Two-player zero-sum games modeled as payoff matrices. Determining optimal strategies using linear programming concepts. Introduction to information retrieval as vector space search: term-document matrices, cosine similarity, and relevance ranking.","prerequisites_within_course":["Game strategies and information retrieval"]},{"subtopic_name":"Specialized applications: chessboard moves and genetics modeling","depth_level":"introductory","estimated_hours":2.0,"description":"Knight's tour problem and chessboard move modeling using state-space representation and linear recurrences. Genetics modeling: inheritance patterns, population dynamics matrices, Leslie matrices in demography, and Markov chain models for gene sequencing.","prerequisites_within_course":["Specialized applications: chessboard moves and genetics modeling"]}]}],"total_hours":45.0,"coverage_summary":"This undergraduate Linear Algebra course covers six interconnected units spanning 45 contact hours across one semester. The course begins by establishing the conceptual importance of linearity and its applications (Unit I), then builds a strong foundation in solving systems of linear equations using vector-matrix methods, row elimination, and rank analysis (Unit II). Unit III develops the abstract theory of vector spaces including subspaces, linear independence, basis, dimension, linear transformations, and the rank-nullity theorem, culminating in inner product spaces and the Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process. Unit IV focuses on eigenvalues and eigenvectors with emphasis on the characteristic polynomial, Cayley-Hamilton theorem, diagonalization, symmetric matrices, quadratic forms, and the least squares method. Unit V introduces singular value decomposition (SVD) as a generalization of eigenvalue decomposition, covering spectral decomposition, rank-k approximation, the power method, and applications in PCA, document ranking, and clustering. Unit VI consolidates the course through extensive real-world applications including traffic systems, circuit analysis, chemical balancing, data compression, word embeddings, Markov matrices and PageRank, game strategies, information retrieval, and genetics modeling. The course maintains a balance between conceptual understanding, computational proficiency, and application across disciplines consistent with undergraduate-level expectations."}
